# Cursor AI Rules for Certio Project

## Project Overview
Certio is an AI-powered legal management platform. The solution uses:
- **Certio.Web** - ASP.NET Core 9.0 MVC web application (presentation layer)
- **Certio.Application** - .NET 8.0 class library (business logic, services, interfaces, DTOs)
- **Certio.Domain** - .NET 8.0 class library (entities, enums, permissions, domain logic)
- **Certio.Infrastructure** - .NET 8.0 class library (ApplicationDbContext, migrations, interceptors)
- **Certio.Tests** - .NET 9.0 unit tests (xUnit + Moq)
- **ai_agents/** - Python 3.11 FastAPI microservice (AI/RAG agents)
- **Database:** SQL Server (Azure SQL for production, Docker for local development)
- **Caching:** Redis (optional) + in-memory

## Architecture
- Clean Architecture: Domain -> Application -> Infrastructure -> Web
- Thin Controllers pattern: controllers delegate to services, < 50 lines per action
- ServiceResult<T> pattern for standardized service responses
- 23+ permissions across 6 categories with CachedPermissionService
- Organization-scoped multi-tenancy with law firm-client relationships
- 4 SignalR hubs: ChatHub, DirectHub, NotificationHub, UpdatesHub
- EF Core with AuditInterceptor for automatic audit logging

## Code Style and Conventions

### C# (.NET)
- Use C# 12 features where appropriate
- Follow async/await patterns for all I/O operations
- Use dependency injection (constructor injection)
- Use ServiceResult<T> return type in application services
- Implement proper error handling with domain exceptions
- Use Entity Framework Core for data access (no raw SQL)
- Follow Clean Architecture layer boundaries (no upward dependencies)
- Always check permissions via PermissionService before operations
- Use IAuditable and ISoftDeletable interfaces on entities
- Validate organization membership on all user-facing endpoints

### Python (AI Agents)
- Use type hints
- Follow PEP 8 style guidelines
- Use async/await for async operations
- Implement proper error handling
- Use dataclasses for data models
- Follow the existing project structure

## Database Guidelines
- Use Entity Framework migrations for schema changes (project: Certio.Web)
- Always use parameterized queries (EF Core handles this)
- Never commit database files (.db, .mdf) to git
- Use transactions for multi-step operations
- Follow the existing DbContext patterns in ApplicationDbContext
- Support both Azure SQL and local SQL Server via smart detection

## File Structure
- **Controllers** go in `Certio.Web/Controllers/` (MVC) or `Certio.Web/Controllers/Api/` (API)
- **Application Services** go in `Certio.Application/Services/`
- **Service Interfaces** go in `Certio.Application/Interfaces/`
- **DTOs** go in `Certio.Application/DTOs/`
- **Domain Entities** go in `Certio.Domain/{DomainArea}/`
- **Web-layer Services** go in `Certio.Web/Services/`
- **Middleware** goes in `Certio.Web/Middleware/`
- **Security** goes in `Certio.Web/Security/`
- **Views** go in `Certio.Web/Views/{ControllerName}/`
- **Static assets** go in `Certio.Web/wwwroot/` (css/, js/, images/)
- **AI agents** go in `ai_agents/`
- **Configuration** in `appsettings.json` and `.env`

## Security
- All endpoints require `[Authorize]` or `[Authorize(Policy = "OrgMember")]`
- Use `[AllowAnonymous]` only for public endpoints (OAuth callbacks, public change notices, WOPI)
- Always validate organization membership before data access
- Use `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` on POST actions
- Check permissions via PermissionService in services
- IDOR protection: always scope queries by organization
- Sanitize all user input via InputValidator

## Testing
- Write unit tests in `Certio.Tests/` using xUnit and Moq
- Use Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory for database tests
- Mock external dependencies
- Follow AAA pattern (Arrange, Act, Assert)
- Test services, controllers, hubs, and security attributes

## Performance
- Use async operations everywhere
- Leverage CachedPermissionService for permission checks (Memory L1 + Redis L2)
- Use AsNoTracking() for read-only queries
- Optimize with database indexes
- Use background services (IHostedService) for heavy operations